Before: the caller reaches through four objects to get a tax rate.

```java
double taxRate = order.getCustomer().getAddress().getCountry().getTaxRate();
```

After: `Order` exposes the one thing callers actually need, and privately delegates through its own collaborators.

```java
class Order {
    double getTaxRate() {
        return customer.getAddress().getCountry().getTaxRate();
    }
}

// caller
double taxRate = order.getTaxRate();
```

The traversal logic didn't disappear — it moved inside `Order`, which is allowed to know its own `Customer`. If `Country` is ever replaced with a `TaxJurisdiction`, only `Order.getTaxRate()` needs to change; every caller stays untouched.
